Here’s a quick, reusable way to model an **optional e‑mail field** in TypeScript.

```ts
// 1️⃣  A simple “maybe‑string” alias (useful if you want the type to be reusable)
type OptionalEmail = string | undefined;

// 2️⃣  A more expressive version that also hints at the shape of an e‑mail address
type Email = `${string}@${string}.${string}`;   // very loose “looks‑like‑email” pattern
type OptionalEmail = Email | undefined;

// 3️⃣  Putting it into an object (the most common use‑case)
interface User {
  /** The user’s e‑mail address – it may be omitted or set to undefined */
  email?: Email;   // same as: email?: string;
}
```

### How to use it

```ts
const a: User = {};                     // ✅ email omitted
const b: User = { email: undefined };   // ✅ explicitly undefined
const c: User = { email: "bob@example.com" }; // ✅ valid e‑mail string

// The compiler will still let any string through (runtime validation is needed):
const d: User = { email: "not-an-email" }; // ✅ compiles, but you’d validate at runtime
```

> **Note:** TypeScript can’t enforce the actual e‑mail format at compile‑time; you’ll need a runtime check (e.g., a regex or a library) if you want to guarantee a valid address. The `Email` template‑literal type above only gives a *very* loose shape (`something@something.something`) to help catch obvious typos while you’re coding.
